{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001033","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Trait/endpoint alignment: Among the fixed candidates, PGS001033 is the only model whose trait_efo exactly matches the target (blood coagulation disease). The other two candidates (PGS001826, PGS002034) target a narrower/different EFO (congenital vitamin K-dependent coagulation factors deficiency), so they are less directly matched to the requested trait.

Model quality signals from the candidate records (per the PRS Model Evaluator overview): PGS001033 provides directly comparable discrimination metrics including PRS-only AUROC (0.634) and full-model AUROC (0.656) with stated covariates, plus a large training sample (n=269,704) and a large European validation sample (n=67,425). In contrast, PGS001826 and PGS002034 report only partial correlation (partial-r ~0.056) without AUROC/R², making cross-candidate performance comparison less direct.

Heritability sanity-check: A trait-specific h2 lookup via get_heritability_records("blood coagulation disease") returned no matching EUR records, so no heritability-based calibration or ceiling check can be applied here; the decision therefore relies on endpoint fidelity and the availability/clarity of performance evidence in the candidate metadata."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004949","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint match is direct across candidates (trait_efo=dilated cardiomyopathy), but several models differ in phenotype specificity (some are explicitly non-ischemic/clinical DCM) and in whether they are MTAG-derived. Per the performance-metrics guidance (prs_model_evaluator skill section: performance_metrics), PRS-only metrics (explicitly labeled univariate/PRS-only AUC, delta PRS/residual R²) are more comparable than full-model AUROC/R², and small full-model AUROC differences are not decisive.

Among candidates with explicit PRS-only discrimination, PGS004949 (DCM MTAG; PRS-CS; ~1.04M variants; GWAS EUR-dominant; evaluated in MAE) shows the strongest PRS-only AUC (0.68, CI 0.66–0.69) and the largest reported PRS contribution (liability-scale delta R² 0.095; Nagelkerke delta 0.029) plus a strong per-SD OR (1.91). Competing MAE-evaluated scores (PGS004946/47/48) have lower PRS-only AUC (0.64–0.66) and/or smaller delta R². The older EUR-only evaluated scores (PGS004861/62) report only full-model metrics (AUROC ~0.70–0.71 with age/sex/PCs), which are less comparable for ranking than PRS-only metrics.

Heritability sanity-check: local EUR LDSC records for cardiomyopathy-related phenotypes show low observed-scale h2 (~0.0007–0.0066) but substantially higher liability-scale h2 (~0.11–0.69) depending on phenotype definition (get_heritability_records for dilated cardiomyopathy returned cardiomyopathy matches). This supports treating liability-scale R² and PRS-only AUC as the more interpretable signals here and cautions against over-interpreting full-model AUROC. Overall, PGS004949 has the strongest PRS-isolated performance evidence in the fixed list, with some uncertainty due to evaluation ancestry/labeling (MAE evaluation) and MTAG construction, hence Moderate confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS002031","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Both candidates are direct matches to the target (trait_efo = iron metabolism disease; trait/phenotyping reported = Disorders of iron metabolism) and come from the same UKB portability study with the same training N (391,124), validation ancestry (selected European), validation N (19,883), and similar basic covariates (age/sex/PCs etc.), so endpoint fidelity and study context do not differentiate them (per skill_overview).

Given the lack of PRS-only AUC/R2 and reliance on an alternative metric (partial correlation), the most defensible differentiators are (i) the PRS-comparable metric available and (ii) structural signals like method/variant count (per performance_metrics guidance). PGS002031 has a slightly higher partial-r (0.1355 vs 0.1307) and substantially greater variant count (6,713 vs 654) with an LDpred2 method, which is a reasonable tie-breaker within the same publication family.

Heritability sanity-check: local EUR h2 records for closely related iron traits are modest (~0.04–0.09). The observed partial-r values (~0.13) are not obviously inconsistent with a modest-heritability architecture, and do not raise red flags about covariate-driven inflation since both models use similar basic covariates and report the same metric type."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000708","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint fidelity: PGS000708 is the only candidate whose trait_reported/trait_efo directly match the target (“Kidney failure” / “kidney failure”), whereas most others are chronic kidney disease definitions (often eGFR-based) or even acute kidney injury. Its phenotyping_reported is narrower (“Diabetic kidney failure in all”), but still a kidney-failure endpoint and closer to the target than CKD proxies (per trait-label guidance prioritizing phenotyping_reported while still valuing direct disease endpoints).

Evidence package vs alternatives: Several CKD scores report similar full-model discrimination (~0.78; e.g., PGS002237) but are more heavily ‘packaged’ with clinical covariates (hypertension, diabetes, BMI, meds), making their reported AUROC less attributable to the PGS itself. PGS000708’s performance is also full-model (C-index 0.779) and thus not purely PRS-comparable, but it uses a more standard covariate set (age/sex/PCs/batch) and has large UKB training (n=223,327) and validation (n=135,300), supporting robustness.

Heritability sanity-check: Local EUR LDSC records for kidney-failure-like self-report show very low observed-scale h2 (~0.003–0.005) with much higher liability-scale estimates, consistent with a low-prevalence endpoint where PRS-only metrics may look modest and full-model metrics can be influenced by covariates. This supports preferring the most endpoint-aligned, well-powered model with relatively cleaner covariate adjustment rather than over-interpreting small differences in full-model AUROC across CKD-adjacent candidates."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000077","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint fidelity: PGS000077 is a direct match to the target (trait_efo = lymphoid leukemia; phenotyping_reported = Incident Lymphocytic Leukemia), aligning with the guidance that phenotyping_reported is the primary endpoint-fidelity field (prs_model_evaluator trait-labels section). Competing high-AUC options (e.g., PGS000874) are for chronic lymphocytic leukemia and further restricted to individuals with a family history, which is a narrower endpoint than generic lymphoid leukemia risk.

Performance/packaging: Many candidates report only full-model AUROC (often with age/sex/PCs), which can be influenced by covariates; among the direct lymphoid-leukemia EFO matches, PGS000077 has strong discrimination (full-model AUROC 0.719; C-index 0.735) in a very large European evaluation (n=391,338). PGS000788 shows slightly higher full-model AUROC (0.738) but is explicitly a reweighting of PGS000077 (“inverse variant weights”) rather than an independently derived score, and it reports an unusually large R² (0.415) that is difficult to interpret as PRS-only given the covariate-adjusted full model context; thus PGS000077 is the cleaner primary choice.

Heritability sanity-check: Local EUR LDSC records for “Lymphoid leukaemia” show very low observed-scale h2 (~0.0014–0.0046) but much higher liability-scale h2 estimates (~0.07–0.40), indicating substantial uncertainty/scale effects for this rare disease; this supports being cautious about over-interpreting very large reported variance-explained values and favors selecting the most endpoint-faithful, cleanly presented direct model among the candidates."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS005217","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint fidelity: Target is peripheral vascular disease; among candidates, PGS005217 is for incident peripheral artery disease (a close, clinically coherent vascular endpoint), whereas PGS001843/PGS002055 are portability-framework scores for an unspecified PVD label with only weak partial-correlation reporting, and PGS005158 is restricted to PAD in type 2 diabetes (population/endpoint restriction). Per the trait-label guidance (read_skill_section: trait_labels), direct diagnosis/incidence endpoints from disease-focused studies with richer evidence can outweigh exact-but-vague portability labels.

Evidence strength/validation: PGS005217 is a modern LDpred2 genome-wide score with very large validation (n=304,294) and reported discrimination (C-index/full-model AUC 0.731) plus a strong HR (1.66), and it is evaluated across multiple ancestries, supporting robustness/transportability. By contrast, PGS001843/PGS002055 provide only partial-r (~0.015–0.018) in the selected European validation and no AUC/R2, limiting interpretability.

Heritability sanity-check: Local EUR h2 records for related endpoints (e.g., UKBB ‘Peripheral artery disease’ and self-reported ‘peripheral vascular disease’) show modest observed-scale h2 (~0.001–0.009; liability-scale higher), consistent with expecting modest PRS-only signal and making the portability candidates’ tiny partial-r unsurprising; thus, the stronger disease-focused evidence package for PGS005217 is more compelling despite reliance on full-model discrimination (get_heritability_records: peripheral vascular disease; read_skill_section: skill_overview/trait_labels)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004163","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS evaluation framework (read_skill_section: skill_overview), I prioritized (i) endpoint fidelity to rheumatoid arthritis, (ii) availability/cleanliness of performance evidence, and (iii) validation scale/robustness.

Among the direct rheumatoid arthritis candidates, PGS004163 (UKBB-EUR.MultiPRS.CV) has the strongest reported discrimination and variance explained in a large European evaluation (AUROC 0.7469; R² 0.1376; n=90,274; covariates listed as 0 in the performance record, suggesting the reported metrics are largely PGS-driven rather than covariate-packaged). It outperforms other RA models in the same 2023 evaluation set (e.g., LDpred2.CV PGS004049 AUROC 0.7283; R² 0.1220) and is supported by a consistent study family with comparable reporting.

Heritability sanity-check (get_heritability_records for rheumatoid arthritis) shows EUR h2_liability estimates around ~0.10–0.14 in GWAS Atlas records, making an R² on the order of ~0.14 plausible for a strong score without implying an obviously impossible result; UKB ICD/self-report-derived h2 records are much lower and highlight phenotype-definition sensitivity, so I keep confidence at Moderate rather than High."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000136","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All candidates except PGS002785 are direct endpoint matches for schizophrenia (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all schizophrenia). PGS002785 is not endpoint-aligned (phenotyping is cognitive function), and its partial R²=0.008 is not directly interpretable for schizophrenia risk prediction.

Among the direct-match schizophrenia scores (PGS000133/134/135/136), none report PRS-only AUROC/R²; only full-model AUROC and/or per-SD OR are available, so per the performance-metrics guidance, full-model AUROC is not a clean cross-model discriminator and effect sizes can help. PGS000136 has a strong per-SD OR (1.7, supportive of meaningful PRS signal) and a higher full-model AUROC (0.64) than PGS000133/134 (0.60), while also having a substantially larger validation sample than PGS000135 (n=18,461 vs 9,569), whose AUROC=0.74 is an outlier based on a much smaller validation set.

Heritability sanity-check: local EUR schizophrenia h2 records are substantial (e.g., liability-scale h2 around ~0.24–0.31 in multiple GWAS-atlas entries), consistent with schizophrenia being highly polygenic; this supports expecting non-trivial PRS signal but does not rescue the lack of PRS-only metrics here. Given the evidence available, PGS000136 is the most defensible choice, but still sub-optimal due to reliance on full-model AUROC rather than PRS-only performance."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000461","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint fidelity: PGS000461 is a direct match to the target (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all squamous cell carcinoma), whereas several candidates are for esophageal cancer or other squamous cell carcinoma sites (head & neck, lung) and one is survivorship-specific (PGS005209), which the trait-label guidance flags as a weaker match for generic disease risk (prs_model_evaluator: trait_labels).

Within the direct-match SCC models, PGS000461 has the strongest reported discrimination and risk-gradient among the SCC options in this list (full-model AUROC 0.599 with Nagelkerke R² 0.0304; OR top 1% vs rest 3.51), outperforming other SCC PRSweb variants (e.g., PGS000460 AUROC 0.523; PGS000462/464/466/468 AUROC ~0.57–0.575; PGS000463/465/467 AUROC ~0.583–0.593; PGS000731 AUROC 0.605 but with unknown covariates and no training N reported).

Heritability sanity-check: local EUR LDSC records for squamous cell carcinoma show low observed-scale h2 (~0.0017–0.0032) but substantially higher liability-scale h2 (~0.13–0.23), consistent with modest PRS performance and supporting that AUROC ~0.60 is plausible without implying covariate leakage; thus selection is driven by best direct-match evidence among candidates rather than expecting very high discrimination (get_heritability_records: squamous cell carcinoma)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint fidelity: Target is uterine cancer; per trait-label guidance, endometrial carcinoma is the clinically dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma and is therefore a strong direct match when better supported. PGS003381 is for uterine endometrial carcinoma (phenotyping_reported matches a direct diagnosis endpoint), aligning well with the target umbrella.

Comparative evidence within the fixed list: Among uterine/endometrial-aligned candidates, PGS003381 shows the strongest reported discrimination (full_model_auc=0.761) with large European evaluation (n=144,479) and a genome-wide method (lassosum; 529,365 variants). The exact-label uterine cancer option PGS001795 has much lower PGS-only R2 (0.001948) and lower AUROC (0.66). The older uterine-cancer-labeled PRSweb scores (PGS000541/PGS000542) have substantially lower AUROC (~0.57) and small R2 (~0.013) in their reported setting.

Heritability sanity-check: Local EUR h2 records for related uterine/endometrial cancer self-report show low observed h2 (e.g., h2_liability ~0.0116 for self-reported uterine/endometrial cancer), consistent with modest standalone PRS signal; thus, preference is given to the best-supported, genome-wide model with strong validation rather than sparse-hit scores."}
